SnapShare Photo App Scaling Plan

1. Overview

SnapShare is a photo-sharing application where users upload photos and browse a feed containing photos from people they follow. This document estimates the expected workload and proposes an architecture that can support high traffic, reliable photo uploads, and efficient feed delivery.

2. Assumptions

The scaling estimates use the following assumptions:

- Registered users: 10,000,000.
- Daily active users: 10% of registered users.
- Each daily active user uploads one photo per day.
- Each daily active user views 50 feed pages per day.
- Each original photo is 2 MB.
- Each photo generates one thumbnail of 50 KB.
- A day contains 86,400 seconds.
- Peak feed traffic is five times the average feed traffic.
- All uploaded photos and their thumbnails are retained for one year.
- Storage uses decimal units: 1 GB = 1,000 MB and 1 TB = 1,000 GB.
- Feed views represent requests for feed pages, not individual photo downloads.
- The estimates represent average and peak request rates; actual traffic may vary by time of day.
- Storage estimates exclude database metadata, backups, replication overhead, and other operational data.

3. Traffic and Storage Calculations

3.1 Daily Active Users

Daily active users are 10% of the total registered users.

DAU = 10,000,000 × 0.10

Daily active users = 1,000,000

3.2 Uploads Per Second

Each daily active user uploads one photo per day.

Daily uploads = 1,000,000 × 1

Daily uploads = 1,000,000 photos.

Average uploads per second:

Uploads/sec = 1,000,000 ÷ 86,400

Average uploads/sec ≈ 11.57

The system should therefore handle approximately 12 photo uploads per second on average, with additional capacity for traffic spikes.

3.3 Feed Views Per Second

Each daily active user views 50 feed pages per day.

Daily feed views = 1,000,000 × 50

Daily feed views = 50,000,000.

Average feed views per second:

Feed views/sec = 50,000,000 ÷ 86,400

Average feed views/sec ≈ 578.70

Peak feed views per second use a multiplier of five:

Peak feed views/sec = 578.70 × 5

Peak feed views/sec ≈ 2,893.52

The application should therefore be designed to handle approximately 2,894 feed-page requests per second at the estimated peak, before accounting for additional capacity for resilience and growth.

3.4 Photo Storage Per Year

Each original photo occupies 2 MB.

Daily original photo storage:

1,000,000 × 2 MB = 2,000,000 MB = 2 TB.

Annual original photo storage:

2 TB × 365 = 730 TB per year.

Each thumbnail occupies 50 KB.

Daily thumbnail storage:

1,000,000 × 50 KB = 50,000,000 KB = 50 GB.

Annual thumbnail storage:

50 GB × 365 = 18.25 TB per year.

Total annual image storage:

730 TB + 18.25 TB = 748.25 TB per year.

This estimate assumes that every original photo and thumbnail is retained for the entire year. It excludes backups, replication, and metadata. Actual capacity requirements will be higher when these are included.

4. Is SnapShare Read-Heavy or Write-Heavy?

SnapShare is a read-heavy system because users view 50 feed pages for every photo they upload.

Daily feed views = 50,000,000.

Daily uploads = 1,000,000.

Read-to-upload ratio = 50,000,000 ÷ 1,000,000 = 50:1.

This means the system must prioritize fast feed retrieval and efficient delivery of photos. A CDN will serve frequently requested images, a cache will reduce repeated database queries, and a database read replica will distribute read traffic away from the primary database. Uploads must also remain reliable, but thumbnail generation should happen asynchronously so that users do not have to wait for image processing to finish.

